{"author":{"id":"2462ded6f56967dde059994c39a506c79e39e5dfd10c5a07f07a23a5652a8353"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1773251345,"nonce":"VhYG51JsfzWPqIY2qSBWrgMG+iQ=","title":"cmd_accept: detect unresolved merge conflicts after rebase","message":"When `cmd_accept` auto-rebases a branch onto master, it does not verify the merge result is clean. If the rebase leaves conflict markers (`\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c`), the merge proceeds and commits broken code to master.\n\n## How it happened\n\n`cmd_accept` for issue fd0dd2e auto-rebased onto master (which had just merged c38e9a4). The rebase reported success (exit code 0) but left `\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c` / `=======` / `\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e\u003e` markers in `host/watcher.py`. The merge committed these markers to master, crashing the watcher with `SyntaxError`.\n\n## Fix\n\nAfter a successful rebase+merge, before committing:\n\n1. Run `git diff --check` on the merge result to detect conflict markers\n2. Or grep for `\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c\u003c` in changed files\n3. If markers found: abort the merge, report the conflict to the user via Telegram, set status to `error:merge-conflict`\n\n## Files\n\n- `host/cli.py`: `cmd_accept()` — add post-merge conflict marker detection\n- `tests/test_accept_reject.py`: add test for conflict marker detection","files":null}]}